Subject: POLI: Arrow's Theorem

I asked:
> . . . .  Do we need to discuss Arrow's Theorem?

and Scott Meeks quite reasonably asked
> How 'bout a brief statement of the theorem and see where it goes from
> there?

Kenneth Arrow showed in 1951 that no voting method can satisfy all of these
criteria (which I list in order of desirability):

- Consistency:  If all voters prefer A to B, then the aggregate prefers A to
B. 

- Non-dictatorship:  There is no one voter whose preferences are always
reflected in the aggregate.  (Note that this condition is much weaker than
"one man one vote".)

- Independence of irrelevant alternatives:  The relative ranking of A and B
is not changed by the addition or deletion of option C.

- Completeness:  All possible choices are ranked.

