Date: Wed, 17 Feb 93 23:23:39 CST
From: david director friedman <ddfr@midway.uchicago.edu>
Subject: KIDS: Rearing and producing

David Lubkin asks about child rearing practices. I have two
children--a sixteen year old son by my first marriage (who sometimes
reads this list) and a two year old daughter by my second (and
current) marriage. My son was reared by my ex-wife, but had and
continues to have a good deal of contact with me.

One thing I did with both when they were small children was using
illogical arguments as a game, so that they would understand the
underlying logic. "This is your hand" (pointing at foot). (small
child denies it). Then why is it attached to your arm?" (pointing at
leg). (small child denies it). "Of course it is your arm--see, it's
attached to your hand." Small children, in my limited experience,
catch on to such circular arguments very rapidly, and find them at
least mildly entertaining.

When my son was substantially older, I made a policy (as with most
people I interact with) of expecting him to be willing to defend
assertions, and not of accepting "it's my opinion" as an adequate
answer. My impression was that his mother and stepfather did not
follow a similar policy. Either he caught on and approved, or he is
very forgiving--we are still friends. Of course, I and my current
wife considered ourselves bound by the same rules.

I have made it obvious to my son that I do not think there is any
strong presumption that what they teach you in school is
true--although I am not sure adolescents need anyone to tell them
that. Off hand I do not remember any religious conversations, but our
views seem similar.

On which subject ... . So far as I can remember, my parents never
discussed their religious views with me; we celebrated Christmas in a
conventional, although not particularly religious, fashion, and
occasional jewish holidays with relatives. As a small child I took
for granted a vague version of the religious beliefs of the
surrounding society. At about eight or ten I concluded (via an
Occam's razor argument which I now think mistaken) that it was more
likely than not that God did not exist, and mentioned this conclusion
to my father. He told me that he agreed.

So far as propagandizing, I have given my son both of my books; I
think he has read them both. So far as education, my son's was mostly
under his mother's control, and my daughter is not yet of school age.
I have considered home schooling, but would probably only do it if
someone else I trusted was doing the organizing--I do not want to
spend the time, and am not sure I have the talents, for organizing a
group of parents, and I think it is a good idea for children to spend
a fair amount of time interacting with other children.

Tom Morrow writes about sperm donation. This raises an interesting
question. How do people feel about their moral responsibilities to
their biological children?

My own feeling is that I would be reluctant to donate to a sperm
bank, because I would have no control over how "my" child would be
brought up. I feel strongly that it is my job to protect and take
care of my children, and that therefore I ought not to produce
children unless I can be reasonably confident that they will be
brought up as I would wish. That would limit me, I think, to donating
to a couple I knew well enough to trust them as the parents of my kid.

There are some obvious counterarguments, starting with the idea that
imperfect existence is better than non-existence. But none of them
answer my gut feelings. Do others share them, or not?

