Subject: LAW: smoke

Anton asks for an illustration of something I said with regard to smoking:

>>Showing harm is not sufficent.  You have to
>>show lack of an overriding right to smoke.

Here's an example:  I am allergic to cigarette smoke.  Nonetheless, I enter
a restaurant I know to allow smoking, sit, and begin eating.  Someone at
the next table lights up, and I break out in hives.  Infuriated by this
harm, I throw a glass of water at the smoker.  "I'll sue!" I scream, "You
HARMED me!"

She cooly replies: "The owner of this restaurant gave me the right to
smoke, and you knew as much when you came onto his property.  Your harm is
thus insufficient to give rise to a right to sue; I have an overriding
right to smoke.  Indeed, you have harmed ME by throwing water at me.  I'll
see you in court."




