Date: Thu, 18 Feb 93 00:43:01 CST
From: twb3@midway.uchicago.edu (Tom Morrow)
Subject: KIDS: Rearing and producing

>How do people feel about their moral responsibilities to
>their biological children?
>My own feeling is that I would be reluctant to donate to a sperm
>bank, because I would have no control over how "my" child would be
>brought up.

>David Friedman

I did not and do not feel compelled to control the upbringing of my
biological children.  It is enough for me that their legal parents are
highly motivated to take good care of my kids.  Artificial insemination is
expensive, difficult, and emotionally draining.  People do not undertake it
lightly.  I would have been much less willing to donate sperm if I had
feared that my children would be neglected or harmed by their legal
parents.  Instead, I am reasonably sure that the legal parents of my
offspring are well off, educated, motivated, and caring.  

I am also reassured by the thought that my kids will, if they carry many of
my own genetic traits, be well endowed to survive and thrive even if they
face a less than ideal environment.  It is fun to have my genes, and I am
happy to give someone else a chance to try them out.  

I also feel no compulsion to control my childrens' upbringing because I
believe that they must ultimately control their own fates.  I don't even
expect to be able to *control* the upbringing of the children I plan to
keep!  Certainly I'll have a bigger impact on them then on my kids who are
raised by others, but it is a question of degree, not principle.


