From: starr@genie.slhs.udel.edu
Subject: POLI: Elected LPers

If any of you have read libernet or libernet-d after the split, then you've
seen me argue with those who want the LP to try to elect its candidates
and nothing else.  I won't start the debate here, but I get a catch in
my throat every time someone mentions the 4 LPers in the New Hampshire
State Legislature, and I'll tell you why:

That's the 3rd or 4th largest legislature in the world!  New Hampshire
has 3 or 4 hundred state legislators.  Their constituents number in the
thousands, not the millions, as they do in most other US States.

So, the 4 LPers in the NH State House only constitue about 1% of the
legislature.  Big deal.  We have City Council members and County
Supervisors from the LP here in California with more constituents and
a greater fraction of the power of the legislative bodies they are part
of.

There are benefits from their accompishments, to be sure.  One is that
small government suffers from much less of the problems of information
costs as does its big counterpart.  My experience of the past 26 hours
bears this out:

Last night (Monday) at about 12:00 a.m., I noticed that two cop cars
had pulled someone over in front of my house.  They had their lights
flashing and radios turned up quite loudly.  I left my house and walked
over to see what was going on.  When I arrived across the street (keeping
a safe distance), one of the cops turned and asked me if I "had some
business."  He told me to walk away unless I did.

>From his manner, I got the impression that telling him that my business
was that I live here and that he works for me wouldn't be prudent.  Since
I have longer-than-conventional hair and a beard, and was wearing a
black leather motorcycle jacket, he may have thought I was fair game if
I rocked the boat.

I went inside my house and called the police station, to check if we
still had the same police chief I thought we had.  We did.  I asked what
time he got in in the morning, and decided I'd call him then.

You see, Chief Murdo used to be my Coach Murdo when I played little league
baseball, and I was one of his two favorite players.  We've been personal
friends for years.  I called him up today and asked him what his boys
did that they didn't want eyewitnesses to, when martial had been
declared, etc.  He was very helpful, agreed that they did work for me,
and promised to find out what went on and call me back.

It's only because I live in Albany, pop. 15,000, and have since I was 5,
that I could do something like that.  Those of you who live in other
cities consider what success you would have calling the police chief
to complain about the conduct of his officers.  Also, one of our
City Councilmen is the father of one of my best friends from High School.
We're on friendly terms.

When government is small, such feedback through personal relationships is
possible.  It's not possible in the nearby cities of Berkeley, Oakland,
San Francisco, etc.  I've seen Berkeley cops confiscate video cameras
and bust their owners just for taping a riot they were involved in.  The
cops of Oakland have confiscated the cars of johns for trying to hire
prostitutes, and those of San Francisco have done it to people trying to
buy marijuana (I think I got that backwards - both happened, but in the
opposite cities: johns in SF, potheads in O-town).

Consider that the mayor of Oakland, Elihu Harris, believes that marijuana
should be treated like alcohol.  Evidently, he hasn't any control over
his cops.  SF's Vice Squad is notoriously bad and unaccountable - when
the SFLP put an initiative on the ballot to abolish it, the bookstore
that served as their HQ got raided, with much inventory ruined in the
process, and LSD "discovered."  The guy who led the raid is still the
fuhrer of the Vice Squad.

