Date:     Thu, 18 Feb 93 10:48:58 GMT
From: starr@genie.slhs.udel.edu
Subject: LAW: Coase & Justification

Tom presses me to take Coase's work in "The Problem of Social Cost"
into account in my take on law.

Basically, I lean towards the critique of it of Cordato and Epstein -
"Welfare Economics & Externalities" & "A Theory of Strict Liability,"
respectively.  Coase's thesis requires the standard of social cost,
which I reject for reasons fairly well articulated by Cordato, for one
thing.  For another, there are distributive consequences to the initial
determination of rights which are important.  This is on top of the
fact that there is no such thing as a world with zero transaction costs.

I also stand by my other objection: Tom's abusing the term "statism."
While I think we agree about the relative justification and its basis
in methodological individualism, Statism remains the doctrine that it's
correct to initiate force/fraud.  Merely because someone accepts this
doctrine doesn't make it justified, in my opinion.

Perhaps my real objection here is the way "justification" is apparently
being used in an exclusively positive way by Tom.  He seems to be saying
that statism is justified for X so long as X accepts it by means of
express consent.  I disagree.  Justification requires more than that,
although I find myself at a loss to articulate exactly what are the
necessary and sufficient conditions for the justification of a political
doctrine at the moment.  This is a problem I'll have to sleep on.

As for the classification of harms, it seems a shame to throw the baby
out with the bathwater when it comes to crimes and criminal law.  While
I agree that criminal law has many problems, I disagree that this means
we should abandon talk of intentional harms to others by the name "crimes."
It seems that I should just make crimes a subset of torts, rather than
anything else.  Torts being harms to others in general, I'd then make
crimes those torts that are intentional.  This would be separate from
how they're enforced (although I'd argue that only for crimes should
capital punishment or loss of liberty be damages - all the rest should
be subject to monetary damages only).

Here's a good legal question: should the procedural protections of the
criminal law be applied to the civil law as well, and, if so, why?  One
possibility that occurred to me when discussing this with Mark Pickens
recently was that the conviction standard of a preponderance of the
evidence should suffice for cases in which only restitutional fines
are sought, but that proof beyond a reasonable doubt should be requires
for cases in which damages involving loss of liberty (such as imprison-
ment) and/or capital punishment.

I'm misspelling too much, so I'd better go home and get some sleep.

