Date: Thu, 18 Feb 1993 03:38:42 -0800 (PST)
From: szabo@techbook.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: FO: OCEANIA

Free Oceania needs not a constitution, but a business plan.  This
is especially true if it is to be a PPL, but even a minarchist 
state needs to have a strong economic base.  No matter how
much we crave a libertarian world, politics alone will not motivate 
the extra expenses and hardships of living in a remote area, especially
on a ship.

What is economically unique about Oceania?  Not being hi-tech
or on the ocean; there are thousands of hi-tech businesses that
operate on the oceans.  None of them motivate the building of
an ocean colony; the costs and hardships of living on the ocean
are high enough that it is easier to commute from land.  (The
closest I can think of are the fish cannery ships where workers
live in fairly disgusting but mercifully temporary (a few months)
conditions).

No, what makes Free Oceania or any other minarchist or PPL 
"nation" unique is its political environment.  FO should
be looked at not as a government with a constitution, but
as a business providing the most essential services normally
provided by governments, eg defense, while minimizing the
hardships imposed by current governments.

Who will pay big $$$ for protection from current governments?

* They are engaged in a lucrative business
* Said business is banned, severely restricted or
  impractical in other parts of the world
* Said business is not provocative of militarily superior
  powers

Possible examples:

* Free-drug zone (recreational, pharmaceutical, etc.)
* Metanational arbitrage (taking advantage of price differences
   caused by tariffs, regulations, etc. in low-transport-cost
   trade)
* Secure communications
* Tax haven
* Gambling on "things that matter"
* Banned medicine (human gene-line engineering, pre-ischemic
	cryonics, etc.)

Many, but not all, of these services can be provided by a virtual
cryptoTAZ; the FO company will likely operate such electronic
protection services in synergy with its protected physical refuge.

These customers today engage in $100's of billions of business
per year; under sufficient protection such markets might be significantly
expanded.  A 10% defense & police service fee, which is
probably lower than any government or mafia, on $100 billion/yr
brings in a cool $10 billion/yr, which would equip a quite 
substantial mercenary force.  Thousands of seasoned Russian veterans 
and technology, including nuclear and chemical weapons, might be 
aquired within this budget.  

Unfortuneately, some of the most lucrative customers
are also the most likely to provoke attack from major powers,
eg cocaine dealers.  As a result, these customers might have
to be turned away or charged a quite substantial "provocative
risk" premium.  Of course, some of the merc activities 
(eg old Soviet nukes :-) might be provocative as well;
tradeoffs to consider when formulating the business plan.
Some PPLs will have ethical or safety objectives to protecting 
certain customers, and may desire to limit their business
for this reason (eg not protecting mass murderers).

Once the business plan has been refined and the paying customers 
are in place, the constitution follows from their contracts.

