Date: Thu, 18 Feb 93 10:21:45 CST
From: twb3@midway.uchicago.edu (Tom Morrow)
Subject: LAW: Coase & Justification

Tim Starr responds to my suggestion that he take Coase's theorem into
account by saying, in part, that there is

>no such thing as a world with zero transaction costs.

Well taken, but note that you don't have to have zero transaction costs to
apply Coase's theorem.  Situations having very low transaction costs, such
as those that obtain between two parties already in a contractual
relationship, will still prove amenable to a Coasian analysis.  Like
economic models based on the operation of a market at general equilibrium,
the Coase theorem illuminates even those situtations that fail to fully
meet its defining assumptions.

>I also stand by my other objection: Tom's abusing the term "statism."
>While I think we agree about the relative justification and its basis
>in methodological individualism, Statism remains the doctrine that it's
>correct to initiate force/fraud.  Merely because someone accepts this
>doctrine doesn't make it justified, in my opinion.

This isn't what I argued.  I argued that the doctrine of statism per se is
not something that is capable of being justified.  Nor can any abstract
doctrine be justified.  Because I use express consent as the ideal measure
of justification, only concrete applications of doctrines are susceptible
of being or not being justified.  On this view, saying "statism is (not)
justified" is like saying "copper is democratic."  The noun and adjective
just do not hook up.

I will confess, however, that I will often sloppily say "statism is not
justified" as a shorthand way of conveying the fact that statists often
impose their doctrines on nonconsenting parties.

>While
>I agree that criminal law has many problems, I disagree that this means
>we should abandon talk of intentional harms to others by the name "crimes."
>It seems that I should just make crimes a subset of torts, rather than
>anything else.

The standard term is "intentional tort."  I believe this captures
everything that you want to say without conveying all the extra baggage
attached to "crime."


